Derek Ray wrote:

> It is inelegant design
> to create a game in which 75% or more of the game can be bypassed by 
> simply banging on an imbalance in one feature; and thus, farming should
> be fixed.

I agree wholeheartedly that inelegance is the problem. Some (most?) people
like to play games elegantly, but NetHack does not adequately distinguish
between an elegant ascension and an ugly one. Spotting "255 black puddings"
in the kill list is the biggest clue you get that puddings were farmed. You
can't easily define farming behaviour, so creating a new conduct like "Did
not farm puddings" is not going to work, and making the player keep track
of informal conducts is not so rewarding. Removing the possibility of
degenerate behaviour seems like the best solution to me.

